        <div id="measured-differently" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Manufacturer dBA Numbers Do Not Line Up
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Standard Noise Test</h3>

          <p>
            There is no required, standardized noise test for portable dehumidifiers in the US. The Department of Energy test that sets the pints-per-day rating and the ENERGY STAR certification both cover capacity and efficiency, not sound. Each manufacturer measures noise its own way, if it publishes a figure at all, and retailers often copy whichever number they were sent. That is how the same product ends up with three different noise figures on three different websites.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Moves the Number</h3>

          <p>
            Three things move the number. The first is fan speed: a figure taken on low can be several decibels below one on high, and a single number with no speed attached is almost always the low-speed best case. The second is distance. Sound pressure drops as you move away from the source, by about 6 dB for each doubling of distance in open space, so a reading at 1 meter is not comparable with one at 3 feet or 1.5 meters. The third is the room: a measurement in a sound-absorbing test chamber reads lower than the same unit in a hard-floored basement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sound Power Versus Sound Pressure</h3>

          <p>
            Some figures are not even the same quantity. European spec sheets often give sound power level, a measure of the total acoustic energy a machine emits, which reads higher than the sound pressure level you would hear at a distance. A US listing at 45 dB and a European sheet at 52 dB could describe similar machines. If a figure looks unusually high or low, check whether it says sound power or sound pressure, and whether a distance is given.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An Example of the Spread</h3>

          <p>
            The Midea Cube 20 shows how wide the spread can be. Home Depot's listing advertises as low as 42 dB(A), Sylvane lists a maximum of 45 dB, other retailers list 48 dB, and reviewers measuring on high at about three feet reported around 54 to 55 dB. LG's 40 dB claim for the Sonoran comes with no published method on its product page, and Levoit's own product page says as low as 42 dB while its launch press release gives 46 dB on the lowest dehumidifying setting. Danby sheets for one 22 pint model list 48 and 50 dB on one document and 56 dB on another, as our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom guide</a> notes. Treat published numbers as a ranking aid, not a promise.
          </p>

