        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the quietest dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the compressor units sold on Amazon, LG markets the PuriCare Sonoran DT501BKR0 at 40 dB, the lowest published figure we found, though LG does not say how it was measured. Sylvane lists a 48 dB maximum for the Midea Cube 35, and Levoit gives 46 dB on its lowest dehumidifying setting. Thermoelectric minis are quieter still but remove little water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many decibels is a quiet dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The quietest compressor dehumidifiers publish figures in the low to mid 40s in dBA on low, rising to the high 40s or low 50s on high. Independent reviewers measuring on high at about three feet often report the mid 50s. Thermoelectric minis are listed in the 30s to about 40 dB.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I sleep with a dehumidifier running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is quiet enough for you and set up for night use: low fan, dimmed display, and a drain hose or a large tank so it does not stop and beep. Many people run it hard in the evening and switch it to low or off overnight, which keeps humidity in range without the noise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are desiccant dehumidifiers quieter than compressor ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They have no compressor, so there is no low-frequency hum or on-off clunk, and many owners find them less intrusive. They still have a fan, and the makers we checked do not publish decibel figures, so they cannot be ranked against compressor units. They also warm the room, which matters in a bedroom in summer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do inverter dehumidifiers make less noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally, yes. An inverter compressor slows down near the humidity target instead of switching fully off and on, which removes most start-up noise. They are common in Asia and Australia but rare in the US, and the few sold on Amazon have inconsistent specifications, so none made our ranking.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do retailers list different dB figures for the same dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no required US noise test for portable dehumidifiers, so figures depend on fan speed, measuring distance and the test room, and retailers copy whichever number they were given. The Midea Cube 20 is listed at 42, 45 and 48 dB by different retailers, while reviewers measured around 54 on high.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can I make my dehumidifier quieter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Put it on a rubber mat or rug, move it a foot or two away from walls and corners, point the outlet away from you and clean the filter regularly. Run it on low with the humidistat around 45% to 50%, schedule boost runs for when the room is empty, and use a drain hose to avoid bucket alarms.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing a low-speed figure with a high-speed one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier listed at 42 dB and another at 48 dB may be the same loudness if one figure is on low and the other on high. Look for the fan speed next to the number. When none is given, assume it is the best case on low and compare it only with other low-speed figures.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a small unit for a big, damp room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 22 pint dehumidifier in a damp living area will sit on high fan and run constantly, which is louder than a 35 or 50 pint unit holding the same humidity on low. Size one step up from what the room strictly needs, and the quiet low setting becomes the one you actually use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the bucket alarm and display</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The compressor is rarely what wakes people. A bright display and a bucket-full beep in the middle of the night are the usual culprits. Choose a unit with a dimmable display and a large tank, use a drain hose where you can, and empty the bucket at bedtime when you cannot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a quiet unit is an inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Inverter compressors are rare on US portable dehumidifiers. The Midea Cube and LG's US Sonoran are not described as inverter units on the pages we checked. If the specifications do not say inverter, assume a conventional single-speed compressor and judge it on its published noise figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting it on a bare floor in a corner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hard floors carry vibration, and corners reflect sound back at you. A rubber mat under the unit, a foot or two of clearance from the walls, the outlet pointed away from the bed and a clean filter can make an ordinary dehumidifier sound noticeably quieter at no cost.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

